.Im MicroEmacs command|file
.so me.tni
.TH reread-file 3
.XI reread-file - "Reload the current buffer's file"
.XI reread-all - "Reload all files"
.SH NAME
reread-file \- Reload the current buffer's file
.Me $a
.SH SYNOPSIS
.na
.Me "|reread-file"
.I n
.B reread-file
.br
.Me "|reread-all"
.I n
.B reread-all
["\fIpath\fR"]
.ad
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B reread-file
reloads from disk the file associated with the current buffer, this command is particularly useful when the file is
continually updated by an external program. If the buffer has been edited and its name does not start with a
\&'\fC*\fR' then the user is prompted as to whether the changes should be discarded. Also if the buffer has an active
process running in it then confirmation is sort from the user before the process is killed.
.PP
The argument \fIn\fR can be used to change the default behavior of reread-file described above, \fIn\fR is a bit based
flag where:
.IP \fB0x01\fR
Enables loss of work checks (default), if this flag is not supplied then the buffer is reread without any user prompts
(useful in macros).
.PP
.B reread-all
reloads out-of-date buffers. The argument \fIn\fR is a bit based flag where:
.IP \fB0x01\fR
Prompt before discarding edits in a modified buffer. This flag takes priority over \fB0x02\fR.
.IP \fB0x02\fR
Yes to all, discard edits in modified buffers without prompting.
.IP \fB0x04\fR
Reload all buffers, not just those that are out-of-date.
.IP \fB0x08\fR
Reload only those buffers whose file is within the given \fIpath\fR. If \fIpath\fR is not supplied on the command line
the user is prompted for it.
.PP
Note that when both \fB0x01\fR and \fB0x02\fR are clear, buffers with unsaved edits are left untouched (not reloaded)
and no prompt is given. The default with no numeric argument (i.e. \fIn\fR is zero) is thus to reload out-of-date
buffers that have no unsaved edits, leaving any buffer with unsaved edits untouched.
.SH NOTES
\fBreread-file\fR is a macro implemented in \fCtool.emf\fR.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.na
